$project — Select Fields
$project is used to control which fields appear in the output and can also create calculated fields.
db.products.aggregate([
    {
        $project: {
            productName: 1,
            price: 1
        }
    }
])
You can also calculate values:
db.products.aggregate([
    {
        $project: {
            productName: 1,
            totalValue: {
                $multiply: ["$price", "$quantity"]
            }
        }
    }
])
$group — Group Documents
This is one of the most important aggregation stages.
It is similar to SQL:
GROUP BY
Example:
db.products.aggregate([
    {
        $group: {
            _id: "$category"
        }
    }
])
We can perform calculations:
db.products.aggregate([
    {
        $group: {
            _id: "$category",
            totalProducts: {
                $sum: 1
            },
            totalQuantity: {
                $sum: "$quantity"
            },
            averagePrice: {
                $avg: "$price"
            }
        }
    }
])
$sort — Sorting
Similar to SQL:
ORDER BY
Example:
db.products.aggregate([
    {
        $sort: {
            price: -1
        }
    }
])
Values:
1  → Ascending
-1 → Descending

$limit — Limit Results
Similar to:
LIMIT
Example:
db.products.aggregate([
    {
        $sort: {
            price: -1
        }
    },
    {
        $limit: 2
    }
])
This gives the top 2 most expensive products.
$skip — Skip Documents
Used for pagination.
db.products.aggregate([
    {
        $skip: 2
    }
])
This skips the first 2 documents.
Very commonly used with:
$skip + $limit
for pagination.

$unwind — Work With Arrays
Suppose:
{
    name: "Laptop",
    colors: ["Black", "Silver", "Blue"]
}
Using:
db.products.aggregate([
    {
        $unwind: "$colors"
    }
])
The document becomes:
Laptop → Black
Laptop → Silver
Laptop → Blue
So:
$unwind converts each element of an array into a separate document.

$lookup — Join Collections
This is very important in MongoDB.
MongoDB is NoSQL, but $lookup allows us to perform something similar to a SQL JOIN.
Suppose we have:
orders
{
    orderId: 101,
    customerId: 1,
    amount: 5000
}
customers
{
    customerId: 1,
    name: "Vasanth"
}
We can use:
db.orders.aggregate([
    {
        $lookup: {
            from: "customers",
            localField: "customerId",
            foreignField: "customerId",
            as: "customerDetails"
        }
    }
])
Result:
{
    orderId: 101,
    customerId: 1,
    amount: 5000,
    customerDetails: [
        {
            customerId: 1,
            name: "Vasanth"
        }
    ]
}
Think:
$lookup = JOIN

$count — Count Documents
Example:
db.products.aggregate([
    {
        $count: "totalProducts"
    }
])

$addFields / $set
Used to add or modify fields.
db.products.aggregate([
    {
        $addFields: {
            totalValue: {
                $multiply: ["$price", "$quantity"]
            }
        }
    }
])
Now each document gets:
{
    name: "Laptop",
    price: 60000,
    quantity: 5,
    totalValue: 300000
}
$set can also be used for this purpose:
{
    $set: {
        totalValue: {
            $multiply: ["$price", "$quantity"]
        }
    }
}

Real Power Comes From Combining Stages
The real concept of an Aggregation Pipeline is not just using one stage.
For example:
Find the top 3 most expensive Electronics products.
db.products.aggregate([
